    {
      "title": "Supreme Court scraps worker-friendly 1978 ‘industry’ precedent for new labour code",
      "link": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/supreme-court-upholds-definition-of-industry-as-per-industrial-relations-code-2020/article71367986.ece",
      "description": "‘Industry’ under Industrial Relation Code will be interpreted in its ‘own text and context’, says top court; the 1978 verdict had enabled workers employed in a wide range of jobs to have a legal recourse, including collective bargaining, against unfair labour practices by employers",
      "source_name": "The Hindu National",
      "published": "2026-08-20T10:40:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "SC rejects 1978 expansive definition of 'industry' for 2020 labour code",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/sc-rejects-1978-expansive-definition-of-industry-for-2020-labour-code-126082000465_1.html",
      "description": "The Supreme Court, by a majority of 6:3, on Thursday held that the labour-friendly expansive definition of the term \"industry\" in its 1978 verdict shall not be considered for the interpretation of the Industrial Relations Code of 2020.\nA nine-judge Constitution bench headed by Chief Justice Surya Kant also by a majority verdict held that the reference seeking reconsideration of a 1978 judgement by a seven-judge bench on the definition of \"industry\" was validly made.\nOn February 21, 1978, the seven-judge bench had delivered a verdict on the definition of the term \"industry\" while deciding the plea of Bangalore Water Supply and Sewerage Board and expanded its definition which brought millions of employees in hospitals, educational institutions, clubs and government welfare departments under the protection of the Industrial Disputes (ID) Act, 1947.\nThe CJI, who authored the majority verdict for himself and Justices Satish Chandra Sharma, Alok Aradhe and Vipul M Pancholi, made clear tha",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-20T07:03:22"
    }
  ]
